  "documentation": {
    "range": "<em>Malacosteus niger</em>&#160;is a circumglobal species, inhabiting all waters from tropical to subarctic (Harold 1999). Specifically, it is found from Arctic latitudes at 66°N. to approximately 30°S. in the southern hemisphere. It is not known to occur in the Mediterranean Sea (Kenaley 2007).&#160;This species is widely distributed in the Eastern Central Atlantic (OBIS 2014).",
    "population": "There are 500 records of this specimen in 453 geocodes. The largest number of specimens taken in a single lot was 22, which was collected off Newfoundland near the Grand Banks in 1968&#160;(Fishnet2 2013).&#160;In a study conducted around the&#160;seamounts of the Azores archipelago in 2002, 9.7x10<sup>-5</sup> percent of the total haul was<em> M. niger</em> (Melo and Menezes). In 1974, this species was considered to be \"fairly abundant\" in a study conducted near Oahu, Hawaii in 1970-1971 (Clarke 1974).",
    "habitats": "<em>Malacosteus niger </em>exhibits unique ecological characteristics in comparison with other Stomiiformes. It is believed to be the only member of the family that does not undergo diel, vertical migrations, and only occasionally moves up the water column into the lower epipelagic zone (Clarke 1974,&#160;Kenaley 2007).<br/><br/>Diet analysis of <em>M. niger</em> indicates that copepods comprise a major component of this species' diet, with only a small amount of other micronekton components. Copepods account for 83% of prey numbers and 47% of prey biomass, as indicated by intestinal contents of samples taken (Sutton and Hopkins 1996). These data are singular in that<em> Malacosteus niger</em> represents an extreme departure in stomiid feeding. This species has the largest gape of any stomiid (Sutton unpublished data), and lacks gill rakers as an adult, indicating that larger, micronekton would more probably be the preferred dietary choice for this species. The large gape and lack of gillrakers as an adult while feeding on copepods may indicate a secondary reversion to planktivory (Sutton and Hopkins 1996). This seeming discrepancy is explained in that the ingestion of copepods provides <em>M. niger </em>with&#160;the raw material needed for long-wave visual sensitivity, while at mesopelagic depths, eliminating the need to undergo DVM (Douglas <em>et al.</em> 1999). Kenaley and Sutton (unpublished data) posed that when the nutritional state of an individual deteriorates after prolonged \"snacking'' on copepods, it may be stimulated to revert to piscivory and utilize this stealthy visual mechanism in the epipelagic zone where high densities of teleost prey aggregate on a diel cycle.<br/><br/>In a study conducted in 1970-1971 near the island of Oahu, Hawaii, it was determined that <em>M. niger</em>&#160;shows no seasonality in reproduction. Possibly the larvae of<em> M. niger</em>, which exhibit no seasonality, either hatch at a larger size or live at greater depths than those of the seasonal species and thus the former's spawning is not timed to any seasonal fluctuations in food concentration or size distribution in the upper layers (Clarke 1974).",
    "threats": "There are no known threats towards this species.",
    "measures": "There are currently no know conservation measures in place for this species.",
    "use_trade": "This species is not utilized.",
    "rationale": "<em>Malacosteus niger</em>&#160;is a circumglobal species, inhabiting all waters from tropical to subarctic.&#160;There is no use and trade information available for this species. There are no species-specific threats to this species. There are no species-specific conservation measures in place for this species. Therefore, this species is listed as Least Concern.",
